ines per call.\n   Use the graph-navigation skill for the exact procedure.\n3. REPRODUCE FIRST. Before editing anything, write a small script\n   under /tmp that triggers the reported failure, and run it with\n   run_command. Confirm you see the reported failure. If you cannot\n   reproduce it, your localization is wrong: go back to step 2. Use\n   the reproduce-first skill for the exact procedure.\n4. FIX. Make one small edit at a time with edit_file (prefer it over\n   write_file, which rewrites whole files). Re-run the /tmp repro\n   after each edit. One hypothesis at a time: if a fix does not change\n   the repro outcome, revert it before trying the next idea.\n5. VERIFY (narrow to wide). Run the narrowest relevant test first\n   (single test, quiet, short traceback), then the containing test\n   file, then the affected package directory. After every pytest run,\n   use the test-summary skill: never paste raw pytest output into\n   your reasoning, condense it to a short failure summary first.\n   Stop expanding the test scope once the repro passes and the\n   directly related tests pass.\n6. CLEAN AND SUBMIT. Delete any scratch files under /workspace (there\n   should be none), run get_status to confirm the diff touches only\n   library code, then call submit_patch. get_status and submit_patch\n   are free and do not count toward the tool-call budget.\n\n## Budget discipline\n\n- You have about 5 minutes and about 50 tool calls per task. Poll\n  get_status every 8-10 calls. If fewer than 10 calls remain, stop\n  exploring and submit the best patch you have.\n- If the same edit fails 3 times, or the same test fails 3 times with\n  no change, stop and submit the best patch instead of looping.\n- Keep the context small: short reads, short repro output, summarized\n  test output. The context ceiling is 32,768 tokens and a flooded\n  context is a failed task."}, {"role": "user", "content": "\ud83d\udd12\ufe0f Add `strict_content_type` checking for JSON requests\n\n\ud83d\udd12\ufe0f Add `strict_content_type` checking for JSON requests"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Applying the fix in fastapi/routing.py (hunk at line 338).\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"edit_file\", \"arguments\": {\"path\": \"fastapi/routing.py\", \"old_text\": \"        actual_response_class: type[Response] = response_class.value\\n    else:\\n        actual_response_class = response_class\\n\\n    async def app(request: Request) -> Response:\\n        response: Response | None = None\", \"new_text\": \"        actual_response_class: type[Response] = response_class.value\\n    else:\\n        actual_response_class = response_class\\n    if isinstance(strict_content_type, DefaultPlaceholder):\\n        actual_strict_content_type: bool = strict_content_type.value\\n    else:\\n        actual_strict_content_type = strict_content_type\\n\\n    async def app(request: Request) -> Response:\\n        response: Response | None = None\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Edit applied."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Applying the fix in fastapi/routing.py (hunk at line 375).\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"edit_file\", \"arguments\": {\"path\": \"fastapi/routing.py\", \"old_text\": \"                        json_body: Any = Undefined\\n                        content_type_value = request.headers.get(\\\"content-type\\\")\\n                        if not content_type_value:\\n                            json_body = await request.json()\\n                        else:\\n                            message = email.message.Message()\\n                            message[\\\"content-type\\\"] = content_type_value\", \"new_text\": \"                        json_body: Any = Undefined\\n                        content_type_value = request.headers.get(\\\"content-type\\\")\\n                        if not content_type_value:\\n                            if not actual_strict_content_type:\\n                                json_body = await request.json()\\n                        else:\\n                            message = email.message.Message()\\n                            message[\\\"content-type\\\"] = content_type_value\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Edit applied."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Applying the fix in fastapi/routing.py (hunk at line 605).\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"edit_file\", \"arguments\": {\"path\": \"fastapi/routing.py\", \"old_text\": \"        openapi_extra: dict[str, Any] | None = None,\\n        generate_unique_id_function: Callable[[\\\"APIRoute\\\"], str]\\n        | DefaultPlaceholder = Default(generate_unique_id),\\n    ) -> None:\\n        self.path = path\\n        self.endpoint = endpoint\", \"new_text\": \"        openapi_extra: dict[str, Any] | None = None,\\n        generate_unique_id_function: Callable[[\\\"APIRoute\\\"], str]\\n        | DefaultPlaceholder = Default(generate_unique_id),\\n        strict_content_type: bool | DefaultPlaceholder = Default(True),\\n    ) -> None:\\n        self.path = path\\n        self.endpoint = endpoint\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Edit applied."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Applying the fix in fastapi/routing.py (hunk at line 632).\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"edit_file\", \"arguments\": {\"path\": \"fastapi/routing.py\", \"old_text\": \"        self.callbacks = callbacks\\n        self.openapi_extra = openapi_extra\\n        self.generate_unique_id_function = generate_unique_id_function\\n        self.tags = tags or []\\n        self.responses = responses or {}\\n        self.name = get_name(endpoint) if name is None else name\", \"new_text\": \"        self.callbacks = callbacks\\n        self.openapi_extra = openapi_extra\\n        self.generate_unique_id_function = generate_unique_id_function\\n        self.strict_content_type = strict_content_type\\n        self.tags = tags or []\\n        self.responses = responses or {}\\n        self.name = get_name(endpoint) if name is None else name\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}], "metadata": {"source": "programmatic", "instance_id": "fastapi_14978", "repo": "fastapi/fastapi", "base_commit": "94a1ee749e63591e6e275b726a66e07a25f6c407", "files": ["docs_src/strict_content_type/tutorial001_py310.py", "fastapi/applications.py", "fastapi/routing.py"], "symbols": ["Item", "create_item", "get_request_handler", "app"], "n_hunks": 14, "n_files": 3, "stripped_hunks": 0, "lossy_hunks": 1, "test_target": ["tests/test_strict_content_type_app_level.py", "tests/test_strict_content_type_nested.py"], "test_target_source": "test_patch", "synthesized": {"graph_observations": true, "repro_observations": true, "test_observations": true, "read_observations": "patch_context", "reasoning_text": true, "repro_script": "generic"}, "note": "Programmatic replay of the reference patch; graph/repro/test observations are synthesized from patch context, not live tool outputs. Read-file observations are patch_context.", "turns": 26, "generator": "gen_programmatic.py v1", "traj_turn": 15, "tool": "edit_file", "est_tokens": 1860, "split_from": "fastapi_14978"}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "# Coder \u2014 system prompt\n\nYou are an offline autonomous Python coding agent working in /workspace, a\nrepository snapshot at the commit just before the fix. You resolve one\nissue per session: diagnose the bug or feature request, write the\nsmallest patch that makes the hidden tests pass, and submit it.\n\nThe hidden tests are applied AFTER your patch in a clean container. Test\nfiles and runner config are reset there, so editing them here is wasted\nwork and stripped from your patch. Fix library code only.\n\n## Hard rules\n\n- NEVER edit, create, or delete test files or runner config: tests,\n  conftest, pytest.ini, pyproject.toml, tox.ini, setup.cfg, and any\n  packaging or test-runner file. If a fix seems to need a test change,\n  you are wrong: re-read the issue and the library code.\n- NEVER write scratch scripts, repro scripts, or temp files under\n  /workspace: anything left there enters the patch diff. Scratch work\n  goes under /tmp only, and is deleted before submit.\n- Minimal patches only: change the fewest lines that fix the issue,\n  preserve existing style and names, do not refactor, do not add\n  features the issue does not ask for.\n- Never guess an API or symbol: read the code first.\n- Do not browse the network: there is none. All tools are offline.\n\n## Workflow (follow in order, do not skip steps)\n\n1. READ THE TASK. Extract: what fails, the exact error or wrong\n   behaviour, and any failing test names mentioned in the issue.\n2. LOCALIZE (graph first). Call the code_analyzer sub-agent once with\n   the issue text and the failing test names. Then use the graph tools\n   yourself on the SYMBOLS it returns (never natural-language queries):\n   search_similar_code for symbol lookup, get_code_neighbors for\n   callers and callees, get_code_subgraph for the surrounding call\n   graph. Only then read_file the 1-3 files that matter. Skim, do not\n   dump whole files: read_file returns at most 150 lines per call.\n   Use the graph-navigation skill for the exact procedure.\n3. REPRODUCE FIRST. Before editing anything, write a small script\n   under /tmp that triggers the reported failure, and run it with\n   run_command. Confirm you see the reported failure. If you cannot\n   reproduce it, your localization is wrong: go back to step 2. Use\n   the reproduce-first skill for the exact procedure.\n4. FIX. Make one small edit at a time with edit_file (prefer it over\n   write_file, which rewrites whole files). Re-run the /tmp repro\n   after each edit. One hypothesis at a time: if a fix does not change\n   the repro outcome, revert it before trying the next idea.\n5. VERIFY (narrow to wide). Run the narrowest relevant test first\n   (single test, quiet, short traceback), then the containing test\n   file, then the affected package directory. After every pytest run,\n   use the test-summary skill: never paste raw pytest output into\n   your reasoning, condense it to a short failure summary first.\n   Stop expanding the test scope once the repro passes and the\n   directly related tests pass.\n6. CLEAN AND SUBMIT. Delete any scratch files under /workspace (there\n   should be none), run get_status to confirm the diff touches only\n   library code, then call submit_patch. get_status and submit_patch\n   are free and do not count toward the tool-call budget.\n\n## Budget discipline\n\n- You have about 5 minutes and about 50 tool calls per task. Poll\n  get_status every 8-10 calls. If fewer than 10 calls remain, stop\n  exploring and submit the best patch you have.\n- If the same edit fails 3 times, or the same test fails 3 times with\n  no change, stop and submit the best patch instead of looping.\n- Keep the context small: short reads, short repro output, summarized\n  test output. The context ceiling is 32,768 tokens and a flooded\n  context is a failed task."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Notes\n\nAdd support for Exception.__notes__"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Reading rich/traceback.py around the suspect symbol.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"read_file\", \"arguments\": {\"path\": \"rich/traceback.py\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "rich/traceback.py (lines 186-335 of 797):\n186| \n187| @dataclass\n188| class _SyntaxError:\n189|     offset: int\n190|     filename: str\n191|     line: str\n192|     lineno: int\n193|     msg: str\n194| \n195| \n196| @dataclass\n197| class Stack:\n198|     exc_type: str\n199|     exc_value: str\n200|     syntax_error: Optional[_SyntaxError] = None\n201|     is_cause: bool = False\n202|     frames: List[Frame] = field(default_factory=list)\n203| \n204| \n205| @dataclass\n206| class Trace:\n207|     stacks: List[Stack]\n208| \n209| \n210| class PathHighlighter(RegexHighlighter):\n211|     highlights = [r\"(?P<dim>.*/)(?P<bold>.+)\"]\n212| \n213| \n214| class Traceback:\n215|     \"\"\"A Console renderable that renders a traceback.\n216| \n217|     Args:\n218|         trace (Trace, optional): A `Trace` object produced from `extract`. Defaults to None, which uses\n219|             the last exception.\n220|         width (Optional[int], optional): Number of characters used to traceback. Defaults to 100.\n221|         code_width (Optional[int], optional): Number of code characters used to traceback. Defaults to 88.\n222|         extra_lines (int, optional): Additional lines of code to render. Defaults to 3.\n223|         theme (str, optional): Override pygments theme used in traceback.\n224|         word_wrap (bool, optional): Enable word wrapping of long lines. Defaults to False.\n225|         show_locals (bool, optional): Enable display of local variables. Defaults to False.\n226|         indent_guides (bool, optional): Enable indent guides in code and locals. Defaults to True.\n227|         locals_max_length (int, optional): Maximum length of containers before abbreviating, or None for no abbreviation.\n228|             Defaults to 10.\n229|         locals_max_string (int, optional): Maximum length of string before truncating, or None to disable. Defaults to 80.\n230|         locals_hide_dunder (bool, optional): Hide locals prefixed with double underscore. Defaults to True.\n231|         locals_hide_sunder (bool, opt\n[... observation truncated ...]"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Before editing anything, I'll write a small repro script under /tmp that triggers the reported failure.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"write_file\", \"arguments\": {\"path\": \"/tmp/repro_rich_3676.py\", \"content\": \"\\\"\\\"\\\"Repro for rich_3676 (programmatic replay; generic script).\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nimport sys\\nsys.path.insert(0, \\\"/workspace\\\")\\n\\nfrom rich.default_styles import _SyntaxError  # noqa: F401\\n\\n# Reported failure (from the issue):\\n# Notes Add support for Exception.__notes__\\n# A faithful repro needs the exact failing call from the issue, so\\n# this script confirms the module imports and the symbol exists;\\n# the failure itself is triggered from the issue's example.\\nprint(\\\"repro ready:\\\", _SyntaxError)\\n\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "File written."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Running the repro to confirm the reported failure.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"run_command\", \"arguments\": {\"command\": \"python /tmp/repro_rich_3676.py\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "FAIL: Notes Add support for Exception.__notes__"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Applying the fix in rich/default_styles.py (hunk at line 121).\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"edit_file\", \"arguments\": {\"path\": \"rich/default_styles.py\", \"old_text\": \"    \\\"traceback.exc_value\\\": Style.null(),\\n    \\\"traceback.offset\\\": Style(color=\\\"bright_red\\\", bold=True),\\n    \\\"traceback.error_range\\\": Style(underline=True, bold=True, dim=False),\\n    \\\"bar.back\\\": Style(color=\\\"grey23\\\"),\\n    \\\"bar.complete\\\": Style(color=\\\"rgb(249,38,114)\\\"),\\n    \\\"bar.finished\\\": Style(color=\\\"rgb(114,156,31)\\\"),\", \"new_text\": \"    \\\"traceback.exc_value\\\": Style.null(),\\n    \\\"traceback.offset\\\": Style(color=\\\"bright_red\\\", bold=True),\\n    \\\"traceback.error_range\\\": Style(underline=True, bold=True, dim=False),\\n    \\\"traceback.note\\\": Style(color=\\\"green\\\", bold=True),\\n    \\\"bar.back\\\": Style(color=\\\"grey23\\\"),\\n    \\\"bar.complete\\\": Style(color=\\\"rgb(249,38,114)\\\"),\\n    \\\"bar.finished\\\": Style(color=\\\"rgb(114,156,31)\\\"),\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}], "metadata": {"source": "programmatic", "instance_id": "rich_3676", "repo": "Textualize/rich", "base_commit": "2b5d648b2c62960deb6439f4204ed8fa6eb3cf7b", "files": ["rich/default_styles.py", "rich/traceback.py"], "symbols": ["_SyntaxError", "Stack", "extract", "safe_str"], "n_hunks": 8, "n_files": 2, "stripped_hunks": 0, "lossy_hunks": 0, "test_target": ["tests/test_traceback.py"], "test_target_source": "test_patch", "synthesized": {"graph_observations": true, "repro_observations": true, "test_observations": true, "read_observations": "snapshot", "reasoning_text": true, "repro_script": "generic"}, "note": "Programmatic replay of the reference patch; graph/repro/test observations are synthesized from patch context, not live tool outputs. Read-file observations are snapshot.", "turns": 19, "generator": "gen_programmatic.py v1", "traj_turn": 7, "tool": "edit_file", "est_tokens": 1970, "split_from": "rich_3676"}}
