ines per call.\n   Use the graph-navigation skill for the exact procedure.\n3. REPRODUCE FIRST. Before editing anything, write a small script\n   under /tmp that triggers the reported failure, and run it with\n   run_command. Confirm you see the reported failure. If you cannot\n   reproduce it, your localization is wrong: go back to step 2. Use\n   the reproduce-first skill for the exact procedure.\n4. FIX. Make one small edit at a time with edit_file (prefer it over\n   write_file, which rewrites whole files). Re-run the /tmp repro\n   after each edit. One hypothesis at a time: if a fix does not change\n   the repro outcome, revert it before trying the next idea.\n5. VERIFY (narrow to wide). Run the narrowest relevant test first\n   (single test, quiet, short traceback), then the containing test\n   file, then the affected package directory. After every pytest run,\n   use the test-summary skill: never paste raw pytest output into\n   your reasoning, condense it to a short failure summary first.\n   Stop expanding the test scope once the repro passes and the\n   directly related tests pass.\n6. CLEAN AND SUBMIT. Delete any scratch files under /workspace (there\n   should be none), run get_status to confirm the diff touches only\n   library code, then call submit_patch. get_status and submit_patch\n   are free and do not count toward the tool-call budget.\n\n## Budget discipline\n\n- You have about 5 minutes and about 50 tool calls per task. Poll\n  get_status every 8-10 calls. If fewer than 10 calls remain, stop\n  exploring and submit the best patch you have.\n- If the same edit fails 3 times, or the same test fails 3 times with\n  no change, stop and submit the best patch instead of looping.\n- Keep the context small: short reads, short repro output, summarized\n  test output. The context ceiling is 32,768 tokens and a flooded\n  context is a failed task."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Case insensitive prompt choices\n\n## Type of changes\r\n\r\n- [ ] Bug fix\r\n- [x] New feature\r\n- [ ] Documentation / docstrings\r\n- [ ] Tests\r\n- [ ] Other\r\n\r\n## Checklist\r\n\r\n- [x] I've run the latest [black](https://github.com/psf/black) with default args on new code.\r\n- [x] I've updated CHANGELOG.md and CONTRIBUTORS.md where appropriate.\r\n- [x] I've added tests for new code.\r\n- [x] I accept that @willmcgugan may be pedantic in the code review.\r\n\r\n## Description\r\n\r\nOptional flag for `Prompt.ask` allowing case-insensitive responses. Just add `case_sensitive=False` to the command options:\r\n\r\n```python\r\nfrom rich.prompt import Prompt\r\nname = Prompt.ask(\"Enter your name\", choices=[\"Paul\", \"Jessica\", \"Duncan\"], default=\"Paul\", case_sensitive=False)\r\n```\r\n\r\nNow, it would accept \"paul\" or \"Paul\" as valid responses. Defaults to True, which is the usual result.\r\n\r\n\r\n"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Running the repro to confirm the reported failure.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"run_command\", \"arguments\": {\"command\": \"python /tmp/repro_rich_3052.py\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "FAIL: Case insensitive prompt choices ## Type of changes - [ ] Bug fix - [x] New feature - [ ] Documentation / docstrings - [ ] Tests - [ ] Other ## Checklist - [x] I've run the latest [black](https://githu..."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Applying the fix in rich/prompt.py (hunk at line 36).\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"edit_file\", \"arguments\": {\"path\": \"rich/prompt.py\", \"old_text\": \"        console (Console, optional): A Console instance or None to use global console. Defaults to None.\\n        password (bool, optional): Enable password input. Defaults to False.\\n        choices (List[str], optional): A list of valid choices. Defaults to None.\\n        show_default (bool, optional): Show default in prompt. Defaults to True.\\n        show_choices (bool, optional): Show choices in prompt. Defaults to True.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\", \"new_text\": \"        console (Console, optional): A Console instance or None to use global console. Defaults to None.\\n        password (bool, optional): Enable password input. Defaults to False.\\n        choices (List[str], optional): A list of valid choices. Defaults to None.\\n        case_sensitive (bool, optional): Matching of choices should be case-sensitive. Defaults to True.\\n        show_default (bool, optional): Show default in prompt. Defaults to True.\\n        show_choices (bool, optional): Show choices in prompt. Defaults to True.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Edit applied."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Applying the fix in rich/prompt.py (hunk at line 58).\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"edit_file\", \"arguments\": {\"path\": \"rich/prompt.py\", \"old_text\": \"        console: Optional[Console] = None,\\n        password: bool = False,\\n        choices: Optional[List[str]] = None,\\n        show_default: bool = True,\\n        show_choices: bool = True,\\n    ) -> None:\", \"new_text\": \"        console: Optional[Console] = None,\\n        password: bool = False,\\n        choices: Optional[List[str]] = None,\\n        case_sensitive: bool = True,\\n        show_default: bool = True,\\n        show_choices: bool = True,\\n    ) -> None:\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Edit applied."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Applying the fix in rich/prompt.py (hunk at line 71).\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"edit_file\", \"arguments\": {\"path\": \"rich/prompt.py\", \"old_text\": \"        self.password = password\\n        if choices is not None:\\n            self.choices = choices\\n        self.show_default = show_default\\n        self.show_choices = show_choices\\n\", \"new_text\": \"        self.password = password\\n        if choices is not None:\\n            self.choices = choices\\n        self.case_sensitive = case_sensitive\\n        self.show_default = show_default\\n        self.show_choices = show_choices\\n\"}}